  "step_2": "The individual reports that although the probability of getting 0 Euros in Lottery A is very low (1%), they chose Lottery B because the difference in maximum winnings between A and B is only 100 Euros (2500 vs. 2400), and they were unwilling to take even a small risk of getting nothing. The individual explicitly weighs the small gain (100 Euros) against the small risk of a zero outcome and decides to avoid that risk. The justification centers on avoiding the possibility of receiving nothing, emphasizing the importance of eliminating the worst-case outcome despite its low probability.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason prioritizes losses over gains and prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less severe) maximum loss. In this case, both lotteries have the same worst outcome (0 Euros), but Lottery B eliminates the risk of that outcome, while Lottery A retains a 1% chance. The individual’s reasoning focuses on avoiding the possibility of getting nothing, which aligns with giving heightened importance to the worst outcome (loss of everything). The justification mirrors the core principle of the decision reason—losses (or the risk of zero) are weighted more heavily than the marginal gain of 100 Euros. The information considered (worst outcome and its avoidance) and the justification (avoiding even a small risk of zero) match the logic of the decision reason. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used this reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
}

